# EnglishTalker web frontend — environment configuration.
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and adjust for your setup.

# --- Same machine (laptop only) ---
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/v1

# --- Cross-device testing (phone + laptop on same Wi‑Fi) ---
# Replace 192.168.1.10 with your PC's LAN IP (`ipconfig` on Windows).
# Both devices must use this URL — 127.0.0.1 only works on the PC itself.
# VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://192.168.1.10:8000/api/v1
#
# Also add your LAN frontend origin to backend CORS_ORIGINS in docker-compose.yml,
# e.g. "http://192.168.1.10:8080".
#
# Phones require HTTPS for microphone access over LAN. Options:
#   - Deploy to Cloudflare Pages + EC2 (recommended)
#   - Use an HTTPS tunnel (ngrok, Cloudflare Tunnel) for local dev

# --- TURN server (required for voice between different devices) ---
# Without this, WebRTC has STUN only. That is enough for two browsers on ONE
# machine, and fails between two devices (phone <-> laptop, wifi <-> mobile data)
# because those networks use symmetric NAT and have no direct path.
#
# Local dev: start the bundled relay with `cd backend && docker compose up coturn`.
# Use your LAN IP — not 127.0.0.1 — so your phone can reach it.
# VITE_TURN_URL=turn:192.168.1.10:3478
# VITE_TURN_USERNAME=dev
# VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=devpass
#
# VITE_TURN_URL accepts a comma-separated list, so you can offer several
# transports — some networks block UDP 3478 but allow TCP:
# VITE_TURN_URL=turn:turn.englishspeaker.me:3478,turn:turn.englishspeaker.me:3478?transport=tcp
#
# Production: set these three as Cloudflare Pages environment variables, then
# REDEPLOY — Vite bakes VITE_* values in at build time, so changing the variable
# without a rebuild has no effect. See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md §10.
